#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082808 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "54852305-01f3-5036-a31c-d7ddcfd7c100" :question/math-academy-id "e-10717" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{u^2} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{a}-\\frac{1}{1}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082918 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"}] :question/id #uuid "14b4c34b-bbb0-5a1a-b987-b2a60f0e9fc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-511" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac { 8}{t^5}  \\,\\textrm{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{t}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{-4}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-2(0-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082919 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"}] :question/id #uuid "efc8fbd2-c814-5c0b-b781-81829ffda2e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-512" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that, this time, it's the *lower* limit of integration that's unbounded.  \n\nFirst, we substitute $u=x-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty$ when $x\\to -\\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-4$ | $-\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\\int_{-\\infty}^{-4} \\dfrac{1}{u^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082920 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "714c93f9-6170-52a9-849b-2e9d94805c4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-513" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x} }\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{-2} \\frac{1}{(1+x)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\,\\textrm{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\n- For the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[2{x}^{1/2}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[\\sqrt{x}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\sqrt{b}-1] \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\n- For the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{1+x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[-\\frac{1}{1-2}+\\frac{1}{1+a}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{1+a}+1] \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\n- For the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\nIn conclusion, only integral I is divergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71522e6f-7b00-5d83-830e-5e5de40bdf73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288818} {:answer/id #uuid "cbaec9cb-c599-5ba8-ace7-af27043df259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288819} {:answer/id #uuid "092eebfb-3ba9-5e64-96e1-5946b9895a40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288820} {:answer/id #uuid "e46d703b-f925-5221-bda1-c2baf909091a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288821} {:answer/id #uuid "63261215-1e5f-5294-8542-0b1c08bb9fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e46d703b-f925-5221-bda1-c2baf909091a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288821} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1fe9e7b-b0b1-5b9c-b4be-e4a5d4296639" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7047774a-5515-50b6-ae41-3214d72cda4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-101735" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z$\n- ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2(-2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{z+3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a+3}+\\frac{1}{0+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals III is divergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "884d42e7-8c61-5605-b94e-e76d28e3d13f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299187} {:answer/id #uuid "5d99763c-ab4d-5d13-beb2-3a0d6dcc1299" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299188} {:answer/id #uuid "848a41bb-e838-5474-83c1-7c60ee93b484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299189} {:answer/id #uuid "760edbcc-8cc4-5d02-9ccc-cd12a044f6ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299190} {:answer/id #uuid "7c7a9dfa-3a83-582c-bf91-a2e38c5165be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "848a41bb-e838-5474-83c1-7c60ee93b484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299189} :answer-field/id #uuid "cefb5cf4-a995-56d9-b548-800b70a60798" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f259d1c-9ee4-5cec-a652-10655665ae0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-152073" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{x}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-2)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc9c1ece-6da2-541b-96eb-067c970a478c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299194} {:answer/id #uuid "d93c1dd1-fd9c-530c-b462-be1229e94db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299195} {:answer/id #uuid "570b97b6-7767-55ff-8a9d-06fabec17b9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299196} {:answer/id #uuid "0c511ab6-1746-5ad7-8b83-2f3d5e297c09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299197} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4c3b7a-2eb4-5e00-8089-b8f2e9d4dffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c511ab6-1746-5ad7-8b83-2f3d5e297c09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299197} :answer-field/id #uuid "0622968c-8977-5e11-8cb4-e1476c21bc1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa273f5b-ed18-5bd0-b99c-c7db22579ef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-152080" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}+2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}+2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}+2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-25$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}+2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-25}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}+2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-25}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u. \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-25}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-25} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-25} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-25)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{1}{25}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34457adf-8d22-5123-9dae-bf860942cdc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299201} {:answer/id #uuid "99c90756-ac79-5f5d-9f89-007029cd71cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299202} {:answer/id #uuid "7e550597-2210-5f06-835f-865b4c946942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299203} {:answer/id #uuid "9070218c-370e-540e-99af-cbd325962aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299204} {:answer/id #uuid "569140db-2295-540c-83c2-e025fdc121c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e550597-2210-5f06-835f-865b4c946942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299203} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c8c3999-135c-5573-86dd-ea6dca68e4be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b574e06-9e6e-5b6a-8649-78b526fbd886" :question/math-academy-id "q-152758" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}5{x}^{-2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-5{x}^{-1})]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{x}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{a}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-5\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc0076a4-053c-5d0e-a5de-f00509b76469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288860} {:answer/id #uuid "1540e31f-8803-5442-bb93-2f0aac2a50e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288861} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf0e9a5-84b0-5ab4-8c5b-11285ec9c003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288862} {:answer/id #uuid "50b56624-6b25-5280-bae4-4c5cab999c6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288863} {:answer/id #uuid "0a330799-bcaa-52e3-96f3-e8a04f4b2567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1540e31f-8803-5442-bb93-2f0aac2a50e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288861} :answer-field/id #uuid "1090056a-5ddf-55ac-a3bc-d49ea2dde0ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "729d01e4-2732-59fe-897c-1e548a2a6adf" :question/math-academy-id "q-152762" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{8}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{8}^{a}{z}^{-4/3}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-3{z}^{-1/3}]}_{8}^{a} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{z}}]}_{8}^{a} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{a}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{8}}) \\\\  & =-6(0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18727bbe-e2e5-52a9-9f54-2fa8e7a2d0b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299208} {:answer/id #uuid "b88dd8c5-01cb-53a8-b29e-e205e94129ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299209} {:answer/id #uuid "1d15d8a5-9581-588a-8ca0-22f32e4dadb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299210} {:answer/id #uuid "dd94a039-a70d-50e3-9d3a-f8d2e30e7aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299211} {:answer/id #uuid "8971a357-60ef-5a52-acbd-3b80c36c2abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The Integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d15d8a5-9581-588a-8ca0-22f32e4dadb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299210} :answer-field/id #uuid "da8fb055-0d15-5c88-ace6-1b413b879ba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0e170d41-084d-538f-a61d-6c472df013b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-152785" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{z}^{3}}{{({z}^{4}+1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={z}^{4}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=4{z}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u={z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $z\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={z}^{4}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $z$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{z}^{3}}{{({z}^{4}+1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{u}^{4}}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{z}^{3}}{{({z}^{4}+1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(0-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab9f4d5a-0dad-5da4-b12f-cdaba690568d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080611} {:answer/id #uuid "acf39c79-2e47-5dcf-80ee-1ce9d3948db0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080612} {:answer/id #uuid "ae65a49f-c73c-59b2-908f-b09f4816f683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080613} {:answer/id #uuid "b72dc5a5-1d57-5576-be73-94cd28890247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080614} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ef0107-500e-53ae-b8bd-fbdb17eecb37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0ef0107-500e-53ae-b8bd-fbdb17eecb37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080615} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f57b66-6cf9-519c-aa1b-aed4dcac0746" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "273c340f-6b33-5250-84bf-da0aab3f9b25" :question/math-academy-id "q-20872" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-2}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f378c1e-7988-55b0-8ca6-060416c74d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299215} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7cc983-5aab-5f99-98f9-27e883c2e83e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f378c1e-7988-55b0-8ca6-060416c74d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299215} :answer-field/id #uuid "4828f9dc-3bc8-56c2-800a-e0a2223a6451" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c22bb14-f75a-54e4-a1e8-fa4dca43eeca" :question/math-academy-id "q-350331" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}y & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =5{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{y}^{-8/3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =5{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{3}{5}{y}^{-5/3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{5}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{a}^{5}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{5}}}) \\\\  & =-3(0-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "904e63ac-2ccc-5ddc-a207-046c3d71db34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "904e63ac-2ccc-5ddc-a207-046c3d71db34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299219} :answer-field/id #uuid "10724fca-93ff-5869-bff8-17d4fc761b7a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5e4dca8-dc29-57ac-ba08-843dc7c7ea8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-350333" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{\\sqrt[3]{{s}^{7}}}\\,\\text{d}s=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{\\sqrt[3]{{s}^{7}}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt[3]{{s}^{7}}}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =12{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{s}^{-7/3}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =12{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{3}{4}{s}^{-4/3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{s}^{4}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{a}^{4}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{4}}}) \\\\  & =-9(0-1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bea6b7c1-773b-5c39-861d-df7d7d6c4751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bea6b7c1-773b-5c39-861d-df7d7d6c4751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299222} :answer-field/id #uuid "53a277eb-23a2-5da8-b7c7-53aaeabebd20" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27ce0742-f150-56aa-89d0-d602f7e76617" :question/math-academy-id "q-350337" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\sqrt[5]{{t}^{13}}}\\,\\text{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\sqrt[5]{{t}^{13}}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt[5]{{t}^{13}}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{t}^{-13/5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{5}{8}{t}^{-8/5}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{t}^{8}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{a}^{8}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{1}^{8}}}) \\\\  & =-5(0-1) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "747bb286-32c5-5794-8d89-3067b6d6ebad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299225} {:answer/id #uuid "72f8065d-d6ac-5d0a-bf32-5c46f2de19c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72f8065d-d6ac-5d0a-bf32-5c46f2de19c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299226} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecdd8cb4-8959-5cad-9588-3e7a0a2db755" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5404e7e2-ca8a-5dbd-8ad7-53e4bb5ca4fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-350528" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(x+3)^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x+3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+3$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(x+3)^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{u}^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(x+3)^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{u}^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }2{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}2{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-3}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{27}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54990329-8839-5b9f-be72-ecbf4c33446c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54990329-8839-5b9f-be72-ecbf4c33446c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299229} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea3cb50b-f366-5f3c-b40e-9ce1220974a8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a61f8d65-5463-50ce-aa4e-b54f5d35b90a" :question/math-academy-id "q-350531" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{(4t+3)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=4t+3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $t\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4t+3$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{(4t+3)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{(4t+3)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-3{u}^{-1/2}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-1/2}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2401ff7-53d7-5fc9-93dc-af89ac1a8b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288877} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0e29a2-462d-5188-9dec-f006de874f4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2401ff7-53d7-5fc9-93dc-af89ac1a8b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288877} :answer-field/id #uuid "96c69c26-abf4-5aec-b4bf-24a816db0a50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8660f5f1-f7ea-5da0-ac60-28e25b93ef09" :question/math-academy-id "q-350532" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(5\\theta +1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=5\\theta +1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $\\theta \\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5\\theta +1$ is as follows:\n\n| $\\theta $ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(5\\theta +1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(5\\theta +1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{u}^{-2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61687b0-9008-5ef3-a7d4-e1635208a08b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c61687b0-9008-5ef3-a7d4-e1635208a08b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288880} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4fe2876-2127-51de-8836-dd27c832933e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ffe81441-8aee-5b69-8444-b663eb3c8065" :question/math-academy-id "q-350534" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{4}+2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}+2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-1/2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{1/2}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{1/2}}] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d43c431d-1b0e-54a7-b48c-4148f021cef9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299233} {:answer/id #uuid "a80003de-7aa5-5c1e-a203-51119564ddba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d43c431d-1b0e-54a7-b48c-4148f021cef9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299233} :answer-field/id #uuid "610bd97b-4b7c-54b0-9996-f645f37e5e5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d8ff14d-b0de-5272-8574-0c630c74c23b" :question/math-academy-id "q-350541" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8}{(3y-2)^{6}}\\,\\text{d}y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=3y-2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $y\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=3y-2$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-2$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8}{(3y-2)^{6}}\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{8}{3{u}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{8}{3{u}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{8}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{u}^{-6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{u}^{-5}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-2)^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{5}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{32}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6513878f-97ae-56bf-a805-82af099c8116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6513878f-97ae-56bf-a805-82af099c8116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299237} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b9dbd80-74e2-5f3b-b789-70e959eb62bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a81a7145-95d3-5175-8540-457ed8a70894" :question/math-academy-id "q-350545" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{20{x}^{4}}{({x}^{5}-2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{5}-2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5{x}^{4}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{5}-2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-3$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{20{x}^{4}}{({x}^{5}-2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{4}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{4}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u & =4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-3}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-3)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{27}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "850bf373-e368-522c-ac3f-d215b97ab8df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299240} {:answer/id #uuid "0bcd1184-aeb9-5c36-b1e0-b3656321ec13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299241} {:answer/id #uuid "8f569797-b6c9-5f13-ae38-77d15b994b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299242} {:answer/id #uuid "ed05cc4c-9148-5278-a1b1-6d44db5df57b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299243} {:answer/id #uuid "d7307377-eb31-55f9-969b-a0b58e2584d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f569797-b6c9-5f13-ae38-77d15b994b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299242} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a0112c6-1ed1-5678-9317-6c2081fac05f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f1a9684-dc43-5403-9a45-d2b9969dd103" :question/math-academy-id "q-5964" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{4/5}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{x}^{4/5}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({a}^{4/5}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86525533-bd5b-5065-89f0-8788651ce48a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081831} {:answer/id #uuid "996531bc-edb5-5093-b4b8-1db7bbe69f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081832} {:answer/id #uuid "21b23561-bed6-51fb-b64f-574e18912063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081833} {:answer/id #uuid "536796e4-8bd5-5fea-9a7f-68e198cabaca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081834} {:answer/id #uuid "5eca8ddf-a39b-592f-b35e-9899f82240eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "536796e4-8bd5-5fea-9a7f-68e198cabaca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081834} :answer-field/id #uuid "9038bffe-853f-51eb-a75b-c72d158db212" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "998df1b1-461f-5fc4-87ac-c188c53c68e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-6032" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- ${\\int }_{10}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{10}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{10}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-1}]}_{10}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-2\\sqrt{10-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-6) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|) \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a}+\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals I and II are divergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ead26d2-6053-54cd-99fe-048771dcd319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299247} {:answer/id #uuid "ed834307-d51c-562e-9d1c-09da7ae9f234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299248} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6ab626-0cb6-55f4-8f8a-31fd0baf4b4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299249} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a49859-a401-5650-84f7-7c45612d0c51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299250} {:answer/id #uuid "0d49f380-a9ca-5e4f-84c8-2fac0a7fc1c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed834307-d51c-562e-9d1c-09da7ae9f234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299248} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f977b89-5ff1-5470-a706-032247c2ae34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0d42f46-94b6-5bf0-ac2d-2b69e88b9757" :question/math-academy-id "q-80342" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-2\\sqrt{1-z}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{1-0}-\\sqrt{1-a}) \\\\  & =-2+2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{1-a} \\\\  & =-2+2\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "beed96ca-eee6-57f1-9425-964fba906eb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292560} {:answer/id #uuid "3e809a76-295b-5781-86b5-2dbb9b038ca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292561} {:answer/id #uuid "4e5f5d69-1902-50fd-b3fc-84fbfabe1369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292562} {:answer/id #uuid "cf61fc6a-da22-5e24-ac86-dd81e6de1add" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292563} {:answer/id #uuid "b5967994-25c4-5a1c-9821-3b5c46d6cdb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e809a76-295b-5781-86b5-2dbb9b038ca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292561} :answer-field/id #uuid "9100afc8-dba2-5771-9b67-77e4a9c47211" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b00f298-c8ee-513b-9db1-ba9601a75ae2" :question/math-academy-id "q-80347" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=8-{x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=8-{x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & -\\infty  \\\\ u & 9 & \\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\infty }^{9}(-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{u}})\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{9}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{9}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{u}]}_{9}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\sqrt{a}-\\sqrt{9}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."}]}
